          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Asphalt Sealcoating",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/asphalt-sealcoating/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is sealcoating the same thing as sealing a driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Essentially yes. Both put a protective coat of asphalt emulsion or a similar material over the pavement. Sealcoating is the term contractors usually use for bulk-applied commercial material, often sprayed and mixed with sand. Sealing is the homeowner term for pail products applied with a squeegee. The purpose and the prep are the same."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a sealcoat last on a residential driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most residential sealcoats last about two to four years before the surface looks faded and needs another coat. Heavy traffic, full sun, freeze-thaw climates and oil drips shorten that; light traffic and shade extend it. Tire paths and turning areas wear first, so a partial touch-up can sometimes stretch the cycle."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can sealcoat fill cracks in my driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only the finest hairline cracks. Filler sealers can bridge very narrow surface checking, but any crack wider than roughly an eighth of an inch needs a dedicated crack filler first, allowed to cure before the sealcoat goes on. Sealcoat poured into open cracks shrinks and splits, and water keeps reaching the base."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How soon can I drive on a freshly sealcoated driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually after 24 to 48 hours in warm, dry weather, and longer in cool, humid or shaded conditions. Walking is often fine after several hours. Avoid turning the steering wheel while parked on a fresh coat, since it scuffs the surface. Block the entrance with tape or cones so nobody drives on it early."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sealcoating add value to a home?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It improves curb appeal, which can help a house show better, and regular sealing extends the life of the driveway, delaying the cost of replacement. It is unlikely to add measurable appraisal value on its own. Think of it as maintenance that protects the value already there, not as an upgrade."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is coal tar sealcoat still used?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is still made and used in some areas, but several states, including Minnesota and Washington, and many local governments have banned or restricted it because of its high PAH content. Asphalt-based sealcoats are widely available, perform well, and are the safer choice for homes. Ask any contractor in writing what product they use."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What temperature is too cold to sealcoat?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most asphalt emulsion sealcoats require air and surface temperatures of at least about 50 degrees Fahrenheit and rising, with nights that stay reasonably mild. Below that, the water in the emulsion evaporates too slowly and the coat may not cure before it is damaged by dew, rain or traffic. Very hot surfaces can also cause problems."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
